\fs28 \cf2 \'93Quickstart\'94 (diving right in, getting your feet wet)\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 When the app opens, look for the menu that says \'93Templates\'94 go to the menu item that says \'93Open saved Template\'94 and open up a template. You should immediately see an image in a window, which is called the TemplateWorkFrame. You can have more than one of these TemplateWorkFrame windows open simultaneously, which is useful for some things. The TemplateWorkFrame is at the center of things and you\'92ll be hearing a lot about it.\
\
What\'92s a \'93template\'94? The image is the template. The template is the image. And the image is made up of shapes that each have a solid color. The template holds the shapes, and each shape can be recolored. The app lets you see color relationships live when you\'92re recoloring shapes in the TemplateWorkFrame, or when you are just studying the coloring of the template. The big usefulness of the app is that it\'92s all built on a foundation of live color relationships.\
\
SELECTION COMES FIRST\
Understand this fact before you do anything. Selection is the first thing to do. Selected shapes are what you are recoloring. There are a few good ways to recolor shapes. But first they have to be selected.\
\
One of the best ways to select shapes is by \'93hue relatives,\'94 which are shapes in the same hue or nearby hues. This app is all about color relationships, right? And this is an important relationship. By a single button-click, you can easily see all the \'93hue relative\'94 shapes for any selected shape. Its color-cousins. But we\'92ll talk about that more in a minute.\
\
Click on any of the names in the list on the left of the TemplateWorkFrame, and that shape is now selected and shows its color. (Yes, every shape in the template has a name). If a shape is selected, then you can recolor it. And you can also select multiple shapes for recoloring simultaneously. We\'92ll talk about that important feature more in a minute, and how this ties in with a foundation of live color relationships. But first, let\'92s have a little fun.\
\
The MONOTRIWORKFRAME\
The main tool for recoloring is the MonotriWorkFrame, which is short for \'93monochrome triangle work frame.\'94 The first menu in the TemplateWorkFrame is the \'93Monotri\'94 menu, and it lets you open up as many of the MonotriWorkFrame as you want. Start with one. \
\
If you have one of the shapes selected, the will open up a MonotriWorkFrame that shows all the colors in the hue of the selected shape. The MonotriWorkFrame is showing that hue only. (Hue is our basic concept of color, such as a red hue or an orange hue or a green hue or a blue hue; every color belongs to one hue, and every hue has a whole range of colors. For example, a particular brown color will be in some orange or red or magenta hue, and there will be closely-related colors in that same hue. See the section below about the Ostwald color system for a fuller discussion of the MonotriWorkFrame and how the colors are arranged.)\
\
Notice that there are two \'93radio buttons\'94 in the MonotriWorkFrame that you opened. (These are the little round ones, and they control two ways of using the MonotriWorkFrame; the default is the xx). But let\'92s look at the group of three larger buttons. xxxxxxxx In the TemplateWorkFrame go to the \'93Selection\'94 menu and choose \'93Select All\'94. Then xxxxxxxxx\
\
So there are two ways to recolor using the MonotriWorkFrame. But before we get into that fun, first let\'92s talk about selecting shapes and selection methods. Because when it comes to convenient selection of shapes for recoloring, there\'92s more than one way to do it. nd there\'92s a lot of hidden power in the selection methods.\
\
SELECTION METHODS\
You already tried one selection method that only allows you to select single shapes. You did that by clicking on a shape\'92s name. You can also do this by clicking directly on a shape in the template image, but the best way to select one shape is by clicking on the shape\'92s name. If you go through the list on the left, you will see each of the colors in the image as you click on different shapes. And you will see that shape\'92s name appear in the Selection list on the right side of the TemplateWorkFrame. That Selection list on the right side is important; we\'92ll talk more about it soon.\
\
The list on the left contains all the shapes in the image. This list never changes, except for which shape might be selected in it. As you click on the shape names you see the color of that shape on the right. And just below the color display, you see a button that says \'93Shape Color Info\'94. Click on this button and a small window opens with various info and a number of small buttons at the bottom. These are useful little windows when you understand them, and we will discuss all that in detail in a minute. (Note that the info is about one color: the current color of the selected shape). These windows can be \'93rolled up\'94 by using the \'93flatten\'94 icon in the upper right corner, since you will sometimes have a number of these and they can clutter things up onscreen.\
\
We said we were going to talk more about  \'93hue relatives,\'94 so here it is. xxxxxxxxx\
\
Moving along to other selection methods, let\'92s talk about named groups  of shapes. The important word here is GROUP. Look for the \'93Selection\'94 menu in the TemplateWorkFrame. Click on the menu item \'93xx\'94 and you\'92ll see another window open up. This window lists the groups of shapes that are convenient for doing selections for multiple shapes.\
