\f0\b \cf2 \'93Technical stuff\'94 (Concepts of color \'93value,\'94 gamuts, Java names etc)\
\
	\'97 Color \'93value\'94\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 The concept of \'93color value\'94 is something that even many artists ignore or misunderstand. But it may be the most important aspect in many situations involving color combinations or general color use.\
\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 	\'97 \'93Gamuts\'94\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 The concept of a \'93gamut\'94 is another thing that is generally not well understood. To put it simply, if you consider the whole range of colors that people can see, a \'93gamut\'94 is something that is \'93device specific\'94 and involves a smaller range of colors. Hahahaha, big help, right?\
\
What does it mean to say that this mysterious gamut thing is \'93device specific\'94? Simply this: a device like your phone or your printer can only produce a certain range of colors, and each type of device is different. The range of colors that a device can produce is its gamut. And when you go from one type of device to another (like going from your laptop screen to your printer) you are going to be dealing with the gamuts, whether you know it or not.\
\
Here\'92s an example: You create some design on your computer, looking at the screen, and you like the colors. You like the color combinations. \'93Yeah, just right.\'94 You even understand the concept of \'93color value\'94 well enough to say to yourself \'93yeah, I got all the values right on this.\'94 And then you go to print it out, and everything changes. \'93Why is this blue so dark? That\'92s not what it looked like on screen?! Yeah, this magenta too, it\'92s not exactly right. Some stuff got darker. WTF.\'94\
\
Let\'92s talk about a specific color to make this more understandable. Let\'92s talk about the color of pure CYAN, which is a kind of teal color. It\'92s a blue that\'92s going towards green. (Your printer works with CMYK, which stands for Cyan, Magenta, Yellow, Black \'97 those are the pigments that your printer mixes to make all its colors, that\'92s what\'92s in your toner cartridges or your ink supplies). So when your printer deals with pure CYAN, it\'92s dealing with pure toner (in the case of laser printers) or pure ink (in the case of inkjet printers). Whatever the color of the toner or the ink is, that\'92s what you get for pure CYAN. Again, this is because every color produced by your printer is created by some combination of Cyan, Magenta, Yellow, Black \'97 those are the pigments that your printer mixes for every color. Good old CMYK.\
\
Your laptop screen or your phone or your computer monitor is different. They don\'92t mix pigments, they mix light. It\'92s a different way of making color, and it produces a different gamut. In other words, the range of colors is different. And things that might have the same name are different. \'93pure CYAN\'94 on your laptop is different from \'93pure CYAN\'94 from your printer. That\'92s right. You might be looking at \'93different\'94 on your laptop and it\'92s a nice clear, light color. \'93That looks good, it works well, I\'92ll use that color.\'94 And then you go to print it out, and everything changes. \'93Why is this blue so dark? That\'92s not what it looked like on screen?!\'94 \
\
But your printer gave you \'93pure CYAN,\'94 just like you told it to. (Your graphics file told it to). So what\'92s the story? What\'92s the secret?\
